  px_title: "Same votes, different rules — different results",
  px_intro: "I counted the same votes under different rules. The examples below show which results would change. These are hypothetical comparisons, not the rules used in the referendums.",
  px1_t: "More areas voted YES, but more people voted NO",
  px1_p: "Abolishing the RTV fee lost nationally by 546 votes. But YES led in 5 of Slovenia’s 8 regions, against 3 for NO; in 46 of 88 districts, against 42; and in 1,857 polling stations, against 1,198. Counting areas equally would therefore change the winner. The investigations law also changes winner when counted by regions or polling stations.",
  px2_t: "Election-day voters favoured YES on all four questions",
  px2_p: "Among votes cast at polling stations on 11 October, YES led on every question, including by 10,255 votes on the investigations law. Early voting involved 75,000+ people, the second largest number in Slovenian referendum history. Early and postal voters favoured NO on all four questions. These votes, about 9% of ballots, changed the overall result on the two rejected questions.",
  px3_t: "How many extra votes would change the result?",
  px3_p: "547 extra YES votes would reverse the RTV-fee result; as an advisory vote, that would not itself abolish the fee. 23,148 extra YES voters — 3% of those who stayed home — would keep the investigations law in force. A different hypothetical change also matters: add 664,854 names to the voter roll, keep every vote the same, and NO would fall below the constitutional minimum needed to reject the law.",
  pxA_t: "Rule 1 · Give each area one win",
  pxA_p: "The actual referendums add up votes across Slovenia. What if each region, district or polling station instead counted as one win, regardless of size? Here is what happens with the same ballots:",
  pxA_n: "Partial results reported by the media show YES leading on all four questions after the first fifth of ballots had been counted. Cities favouring YES reported sooner. The leads disappeared as rural and early votes were added. Early leads do not necessarily predict the final result.",
  pxB_t: "Rule 2 · Count election-day votes separately",
  pxB_p: "YES led by 10,255 to 208,991 votes among election-day polling-station ballots. Early and postal voters favoured NO on every question. Including those ballots, about 9% of the total, changed the overall result on both rejected questions. All of these ballots belong in the actual count.",
  pxC_t: "Rule 3 · Draw districts to favour one side",
  pxC_p: "Slovenia does not decide referendums by districts. I tested a hypothetical map of 88 equally sized districts, drawn to help the side that lost nationally. On the close questions — RTV fee 51.7% YES and investigations law 50.5% YES in election-day votes — that side could win 45 of 88 districts. Against a 60.5% majority, the most favourable map in this test gave it only 17.",
  pxC_n: "With perfectly equal districts, a side could in theory win 45 of 88 with just 25.6% of all votes, by winning those districts narrowly and losing the rest heavily. This illustrates how district rules could change representation.",
  pxD_t: "Rule 4 · Require enough registered voters to vote NO (Article 90)",
  pxD_p: "Rejecting the investigations law required both a NO majority and NO votes from at least 1/5 of registered voters. The 472,444 NO votes cleared that minimum by 132,971. The voter roll therefore matters too: with 664,854 more registered voters (+39%), the same NO total would fall below 1/5 and the law would stay in force.",
  pxD_n: "Under this rule, voting for your preferred side cannot make its result worse; staying home cannot help it win. The RTV-fee margin was 546 votes, smaller than the 2,913 invalid ballots: 547 extra YES votes would reverse the result. Applying Article 90 to all four questions as a hypothetical check would change none of their outcomes.",
  px_ter_cap: "Share of the 88 districts where YES led. The two proposals rejected nationally led in most regions and polling stations; the RTV-fee proposal also led in most districts.",
