  m5_b: "Of 16 checks, 13 raised no concern and 3 were borderline. None remained unusual after allowing for the number of checks run. The borderline results do not show an excess of round numbers.",
  m6_t: "Find polling stations that differ from the others",
  m6_b: "A few dozen stations stand out. All are small (16–342 voters), and their differences point in different directions rather than consistently helping one side.",
  m7_t: "Are identical results too common?",
  m7_b: "Identical YES, NO and invalid-vote totals recur 38–60 times per referendum, compared with 37–53 expected by chance. This gives no clear evidence of copied results.",
  m8_t: "Can these checks detect deliberately added fake votes?",
  m8_b: "I added fake ballots to a copy of the data. The high-turnout vote estimate grew 12-fold. This shows that the method can detect the simulated change; it does not mean every kind of manipulation would be caught.",
  m1_d: "Adding ballots can raise both turnout and one side’s vote total. This method fits a bell-shaped turnout curve and counts votes beyond a chosen boundary. I used the other three referendums, with the same stations and voters, to set that boundary at 70.1%. Across the four questions, this area contains 4,135–4,651 YES votes (0.89–1.00% of YES totals) and 1,297–1,812 NO votes (0.41–0.45%). It continues the city–rural pattern rather than forming a separate group. Removing it would change any result by at most 0.15 percentage points. The estimate alone does not identify fake votes.",
  m1_c1: "Parliamentary Investigations Act (ZPPre-B): 4,223 YES votes (1.00% of the YES total) beyond the 70.1% boundary; without the zone the YES share moves 50.32% → 50.17%.",
  m1_c2: "Social assistance: 4,651 YES votes (0.89%) above the boundary — YES share 62.20% → 62.09%.",
