  m1_a4: "Turnout distribution and leader votes by turnout, RTV fee referendum: small red tail beyond 70%",
  m1_a5: "Turnout charts of the four referendums overlap almost exactly",
  m2_d: "Added ballots can create a separate group of stations at 80–100% turnout and 80–95% support for one side, with support for the other side falling. Here YES support rises gradually: about 4 percentage points for each 10-point increase in turnout. The weighted correlation, a measure of how closely the two move together while accounting for station size, is +0.27…+0.38. There is no sudden change at high turnout. Only 10 small stations exceed 80% turnout, accounting for 0.1% of ballots.",
  m2_c1: "Parliamentary Investigations Act: turnout × YES share, relationship score (weighted correlation) +0.36 — one gradual pattern.",
  m2_c2: "Social assistance: +0.35; the pattern follows city–rural differences, without a separate high-turnout group.",
  m2_c3: "Local voting rights: +0.38 — the strongest of the four relationships, still one connected group.",
  m2_c4: "RTV fee: +0.27; no separate group at high turnout.",
  m2_a1: "Turnout vs YES share scatter, parliamentary investigations act: one connected group, no separate tail",
  m2_a2: "Turnout vs YES share scatter, social assistance: one connected group, no separate tail",
  m2_a3: "Turnout vs YES share scatter, local voting rights: one connected group, no separate tail",
  m2_a4: "Turnout vs YES share scatter, RTV fee: one connected group, no separate tail",
  m3_d: "Klimek and colleagues (PNAS 2012) fit two groups to a chart of turnout and vote share. Some manipulated elections show a separate group at 80–100% turnout with very high support for one side. The model also selects two groups here, but both sit at 51–56% turnout within one connected pattern. All four referendums look similar (vote-share correlations 0.94–0.96). The separate high-turnout group is absent. Only two stations exceed 85% turnout, with 19 and 21 voters.",
  m3_c1: "Parliamentary Investigations Act: share assigned to the model’s second group 0.42; both groups have ordinary turnout; no separate high-turnout group.",
  m3_c2: "Social assistance: model group share 0.49. The model’s second group covers the more rural, YES-leaning stations within one gradual pattern.",
  m3_c3: "Local voting rights: model group share 0.48 — the same picture.",
  m3_c4: "RTV fee: model group share 0.43; very few stations have turnout above 80%.",
