  m3_a3: "Turnout and vote-share chart, local voting rights: one connected group, no separate group at 80–100% turnout",
  m3_a4: "Turnout and vote-share chart, RTV fee: one connected group, no separate group at 80–100% turnout",
  m4_d: "Invented totals can favour round percentages. I compared the frequency of whole percentages and multiples of 5 with repeated random simulations based on the same data (Monte Carlo). The z score measures the distance from that simulated expectation: all scores are within ±1.3 (turnout −0.0…+0.4; YES share ≤ +1.1; NO share ≤ +1.3). Nothing stands out on this check. With about 460 voters per station, however, the test can miss substantial changes; the self-test below shows this limit.",
  m4_c1: "Parliamentary Investigations Act: whole-percentage turnout z = 0.0, multiples of 5 z = −0.1, YES share z = +1.1 — within the range expected from chance.",
  m4_c2: "Social assistance: turnout z = +0.4; YES share z = +0.4.",
  m4_c3: "Local voting rights: turnout z = −0.0; YES share z = +0.2.",
  m4_c4: "RTV fee: turnout z = +0.2; YES share z = +0.2.",
  m4_a1: "Whole-percentage frequencies, parliamentary investigations act: the observed counts match random simulations",
  m4_a2: "Whole-percentage frequencies, social assistance: the observed counts match random simulations",
  m4_a3: "Whole-percentage frequencies, local voting rights: the observed counts match random simulations",
  m4_a4: "Whole-percentage frequencies, RTV fee: the observed counts match random simulations",
  m5_d: "These checks ask whether the last digit, or the second digit compared with Benford’s expected pattern, is unusual. Made-up counts can sometimes favour endings such as 0 and 5, but digit patterns alone cannot establish fraud. Of 16 checks (two sides × two digit checks × four questions), 13 raised no concern. Three borderline results (Monte Carlo p = 0.005–0.12) showed more 4s and fewer 0s. None passed the stricter Bonferroni threshold of 0.003, which allows for the chance of a false alarm when running 16 checks.",
  m5_c1: "RTV fee — the most-voted ballot — second digits vs Benford’s law: YES p = 0.12, NO p = 0.47 compared with random simulations (2,977 and 2,900 numbers).",
  m5_c2: "Same referendum, last digits of counts ≥ 100: roughly even, with no excess of 0s and 5s (YES p = 0.23, NO p = 0.05). The other three referendums are analogous — see sections 6–7 of the report.",
  m5_a1: "Second-digit distribution compared with Benford’s expected pattern, RTV referendum: all bars fall inside the range covering 95% of simulated results",
  m5_a2: "Last-digit distribution, RTV referendum: roughly even, with no excess of 0s and 5s",
  m6_d: "I used two ways to find unusual stations. The first compares a station with the middle value in its district, flagging large differences (robust z-score |z| > 4). The second, Isolation Forest, compares turnout, the leading side’s vote share, invalid ballots and station size. The first flags 22–40 stations per question (0.7–1.3%); the second is set to flag 30 (1%). All are small, with 16–342 voters. Differences favour different sides, and many of the same stations stand out on all four questions, including one with 19 voters and 100% turnout. This does not show a shared pattern of manipulation. The charts show the RTV-fee question; the others look similar.",
  m6_c1: "RTV fee: precincts compared with the middle value in their district. The 29 flagged (|z| > 4) are small rural precincts with differences favouring different sides.",
  m6_c2: "RTV fee: Isolation Forest scores for unusual stations; the top 30 are unusually small stations, such as a 16-voter premises at 81% turnout.",
  m6_a1: "Outliers versus district neighbours, RTV referendum: scattered small precincts, no separate group at high turnout",
  m6_a2: "Isolation Forest scores for unusual stations, RTV referendum: gradually changing scores, 25 highest-scoring stations marked",
  m7_d: "Copied results could create too many identical sets of YES, NO and invalid-vote totals. I found 38–60 repeated sets per question, compared with 37–53 expected from repeated resampling of the data (bootstrap). In the investigations question, 37 of 38 matching pairs were in different districts. I also compared YES counts between questions: 412 stations had exactly the same count, while 798 differed by one vote. Exact matches do not form an unusual spike. These checks give no clear evidence of copying.",
  m8_d: "I tested the tools on a copy of the real data with fake ballots added. Turnout rose by 5–35 percentage points in 10% of stations; 60–95% of added ballots went to the leading side. The Shpilkin high-turnout estimate grew 12–13 times, from 4,135–4,651 votes to 51,792–59,247. In a separate test, I rounded turnout to whole percentages at 5% of stations. The round-number test’s z score rose only to 0.8–1.6. With about 460 voters per station, that check has limited sensitivity. Detecting the first simulation does not guarantee that every kind of manipulation would be detected.",
